n so don't don't worry  too much about it but the s represents  the kaplan-meier estimate of survival  and if Y is our number of additional  years of interest and X is the number of  years a patient has already survived we  can simply divide the kaplan-meier  survival estimate for the total amount  of time by the kaplan-meier survival  estimate for the number of years a  patient has already survived to get a  conditional estimate and if we do this  in our example the table on the  left-hand side has the number of years  already survived from 0 through 4 and  then we're interested in the probability  of surviving to 5 years so for someone  at baseline at time 0 that's our  estimate of 81 percent of surviving for  five years at four years this would be  the probability of just surviving one  additional  and you can see that these estimates of  survival go up as time goes by and we  can also see this by the plot of  conditional survival estimates where  each color represents an additional year  survived and we can see that these  survival curves are just shifted up as  the time goes on and so it's important  to be able to update these survival  estimates if you're giving this  information to a patient who's already  lived for three years  what now what's their probability of  surviving to five years so just a quick  plug for a little R package I have that  does this it's called conserv it's  available on my github page and there's  a tiny tiny link to the bottom of it on  this slide and these calculations are  really straightforward but my package  will give you these estimates and these  plots and if you ever want to do this  it's a nice little easy way to do it so  that's all I have for you today my code  and data and slides are all available on  github so you can play around with this  it's if it's of interest and just gonna  leave you with some contact information  and also of reference to the original  publication of the kidney and BMI data  that I talked about th