many parametric for  of regression models available for  sensor data but I'm not going to get  into those today so we would do this  using the Cox pH function so similar  syntax is what we saw before using  surfeit and commonly you would want to  do a regression model like this because  you would be interested in a multi  variable model you can imagine that in  survival analysis there are many  variables that are associated with time  to death it easily comes to mind at the  age and sex or variables that are  associated with how long people live and  so if you wanted to say something about  a variable you would want to adjust for  these important confound errs I'm just  showing a simple example here focusing  on the BMI still so if we print out a  summary of our Cox regression model fit  we would get regression parameters and  if we exponentiate those we end up with  a hazard ratio and when you're dealing  with a categorical variable like BMI you  have to have a reference group so one of  the groups has to be fixed at one and  then your other hazard ratios are in  reference to that and a hazard is an  instantaneous probability of death and  then a hazard ratio is the ratio of two  different hazards and hazard ratio  greater than one represents an increased  hazard and an hazard ratio less than one  represents a reduced hazard and you can  see here that both our overweight and  obese groups have hazard ratios less  than one suggesting that they have  reduced hazards of death compared to the  normal weight group and our p-value is  the same as it was before okay so what  really is a hazard ratio though so  they're kind of hard like oh  instantaneous hazard of death well what  does that mean hazard ratios are very  commonly misinterpreted and the most  common misinterpretation that I see is  we focus on our example comparing the  obese to the normal weight group the  hazard ratio was 0.64 and you'll  commonly see an interpretation of this  as a thirty six percent reduced risk of  death but hazard ratios aren't really  risks so we can kind of get an idea of  why this estimate of a thirty six  percent reduced risk of death doesn't  make sense in our data by looking back  at our survival curve and the first  thing to think about is the fact that  these curves are somewhat parallel and  they're all going down so what does a  thirty six percent reduced risk of death  mean for one of these groups and  text and the second thing to think about  is that if we look back at our five-year  and ten-year estimates for the normal  weight and obese groups we see that  those estimates only differ by about 11  to 12 percent and so again that doesn't  really correspond to a 36 percent  reduced risk of death  so this is a common misinterpretation of  a hazard ratio I see personally I think  they're hard to interpret and I like to  use Cox regression models to understand  directions of effects in multi variable  models and significance and multi  variable models but I like to use these  other quantities to convey and for more  information about actual differences  between groups so the last topic I'm  going to cover is conditional survival  so one of the amazing thing that's going  on in camps or research right now is  that treatments are improving people are  living longer and longer we have more  targeted treatments and more  immunological and patients are being  cured and so now it's of interest to ask  the question well does that five your  probability of survival that we  estimated at the time of surgery or  whatever your start time was we  estimated 81% in this example does that  really still apply to a patient who's  already lived for one year or five years  or 10 years you know and they still want  to know something about what their  prognosis is well the answer is no but  we can quickly and easily correct for  this by estimating a conditional  survival estimate and this is the only  formula I'm showing in my talk today and  it's just division so don't don't worry  too much about it but the s represents  the kaplan-meier estimate of survival  and if Y is our number of additional  years of interest and X is the number of  years a patient has already survived we  can simply divide the kaplan-meier  survival estimate for the total amount  of time by the kaplan-meier survival  estimate for the number of years a  patient has already survived to get a  conditional estimate and if we do this  in our example the table on the  left-hand side has the number of years  already survived from 0 through 4 and  then we're interested in the probability  of surviving to 5 years so for someone  at baseline at time 0 that's our  estimate of 81 percent of surviving for  five years at four years this would be  the probability of just surviving one  additional  and you can see that these estimates of  survival go up as time goes by and we  can also see this by the plot of  conditional survival estimates where  each color represents an additional year  survived and we can see that these  survival curves are just shifted up as  the time goes on and so it's important  to be able to update these survival  estimates if you're giving this  information to a patient who's already  lived for three years  what now what's their probability of  surviving to five years so just a quick  plug for a little R package I have that  does this it's called conserv it's  available on my github page and there's  a tiny tiny link to the bottom of it on  this slide and these calculations are  really straightforward but my package  will give you these estimates and these  plots and if you ever want to do this  it's a nice little easy way to do it so  that's all I have for you today my code  and data and slides are all available on  github so you can play around with this  it's if it's of interest and just gonna  leave you with some contact information  and also of reference to the original  publication of the kidney and BMI data  that I talked about that today so thank  you very much  you  you
 

